0.8 SCOPE BOUNDARY (MANDATORY — OVERRIDES ANY CONFLICTING LINE)
• IN SCOPE: the CORE SYSTEM only — application runtime, server
lifecycle, session management, context engine (excluding any
provider-selection logic), chain/pipeline, actions, parsers, file
management, workspace management, project architecture, frontend,
backend, WebSocket lifecycle, in-app streaming (application-side
only), build system, performance, memory management, security,
error handling, testing, QA, maintainability, scalability, technical
debt, dependency graph, documentation, roadmap, task planning.
• OUT OF SCOPE — do NOT review, analyze, plan, or task:
the entire providers/ directory and everything related to external
AI integrations: provider architecture, provider registry, provider
base classes, fallback, retry logic, budget/capacity handling,
provider-side streaming, provider authentication, account
management, provider routing, and any specific vendor integration.
• When core code CALLS into the Provider Layer: document the call site
as an opaque external boundary ("out-of-scope boundary") — cite the
call, never descend into it.
• No BUG/FND/TSK/QA-T may target out-of-scope code. If an in-scope
symptom's root cause turns out to live in the Provider Layer, record
the finding as "Root-cause out of scope" and stop there.
