running read-only helper scripts you write OUTSIDE the project tree
(in a scratch dir) that do not touch project files.
TIER B — ALLOWED WITH LOGGING (record every use in PROGRESS.md session
log): starting server.py bound to localhost with ALL external AI
calls DISABLED or stubbed at the out-of-scope boundary; running
existing test files; opening a local WebSocket client against your
own local instance; pip install into an isolated venv.
TIER C — FORBIDDEN in MODE A:
modifying/creating/deleting any project source file; calling ANY
external AI API (out of scope entirely — cost + privacy); reading or
analyzing providers/ beyond directory listing; invoking
command_runner.py against the host; writing anywhere except
docs/engineering/ and a scratch dir; touching test---results/.
CLASSIFICATION CONSEQUENCE:
C1 requires TIER B reproduction. If TIER B is insufficient or unsafe,
maximum confidence is C2 and issue classification uses:
• Confirmed(C1) — reproduced at runtime
• Confirmed(C2) — code path logically proven by full static read
• Partially-confirmed — some claimed aspects hold, others don't
• Already-fixed — code now contains a fix (cite it)
• Not-reproducible — attempted per policy, could not trigger (state
exactly what was attempted)
• Incorrect — code proves the claim wrong (cite proof)
• Stale-Context — referenced code no longer exists
• Out-of-Scope — root cause or subject lives in the Provider Layer
"Not-reproducible" is ONLY valid after a documented attempt;
otherwise use C4 Unverified + spawn a verification task.
==================================================
SECTION 4 — OPERATING MODES
MODE A — PLANNING (default): analysis + documentation only, per
Section 3. Allowed writes: docs/engineering/** only. Committing
documentation in MODE A is allowed and encouraged
(message: "DOCS(P<n>): <summary>").
MODE B — EXECUTION (only when told "EXECUTE TASK TSK-<id>"):
• Exactly ONE task per change. Never batch. Tasks may touch in-scope
files only; providers/ remains untouchable in both modes.
• Backup: git branch is MANDATORY (if repo has no git → run git init
+ initial commit first; ZIP is a supplementary option, stored in
docs/engineering/backups/, never a substitute).
• After the change: run the task's acceptance tests; if the project
has no runnable test suite yet, the FIRST milestone must include a
task that creates one — until then acceptance = documented manual
verification steps executed and logged.
• Update PROGRESS.md, commit: "TSK-<id>: <summary>".
• Discovering an unrelated in-scope issue mid-task: do NOT fix it;
register it as a new FND with a new TSK, continue current task.
Discovering a Provider Layer issue: record one line
"Out-of-Scope observation" in the session log and move on.
==================================================
SECTION 5 — BOOTSTRAP & RESUME PROTOCOL (MANDATORY, EVERY SESSION)
STEP 0 (before ANY analysis, first session):
Create docs/engineering/PROGRESS.md immediately, containing:
• Header: last-updated, current phase, current task, completion %
(formula: completed phase-checkpoints ÷ total checkpoints in
planning stage; completed TSK ÷ total TSK in execution stage —
counting IN-SCOPE checkpoints/tasks only).
• PHASE TABLE: P1–P8 each broken into its checkpoints (listed in
Section 6), all ⬜.
• TASK TABLE: empty until P5 fills it.
• SESSION LOG: append per session — date, checkpoints/tasks touched,
TIER B actions performed, decisions, and EXACT resume point
(e.g. "P2 — BUG-03 verification: read context builder in
server.py, payload assembly confirmed; next: check
file_manager.py read limits").
EVERY SESSION START:
Read PROGRESS.md. 2. If missing → STEP 0. 3. Announce current
phase/task + next 3 items. 4. Re-verify any 🟨 item against reality
before continuing. 5. Resume from the exact logged point.
STATUS VALUES: ⬜ Pending | 🟨 In-Progress | ✅ Done | 🔴 Blocked |
⏭️ Skipped(justify).
==================================================
SECTION 6 — PHASES (WRITE-AS-YOU-GO: each phase writes its own
document DURING the phase, updating PROGRESS.md at every checkpoint.
Nothing is deferred to the end.)
EFFORT BUDGET (guideline): P1 25% | P2 15% | P3 15% | P4 10% | P5 15% |
P6 10% | P7 5% | P8 5%. Sessions MAY end mid-phase — the resume point
makes this safe. Never sacrifice P4–P6 to over-deepen P1.
READING STRATEGY for P1: read fully — server.py (skipping into
provider-routing endpoints only far enough to mark them out of scope),
app.js, all actions/, config.yaml structure (redact values).
providers/ is NOT read — list it once in the repo map as an excluded
directory and state this explicitly.
--- P1 → ARCHITECTURE_REVIEW.md (target 300–600 lines)
Checkpoints: (a) repo map & module responsibilities (a directory is
"major" if it contains executed code or configuration — vendored libs
excluded, listed only; providers/ listed as OUT OF SCOPE), (b) runtime
flows: WebSocket lifecycle, AI request lifecycle up to the out-of-scope
boundary, in-app streaming (server→frontend), session lifecycle,
(c) context builder & context engine (excluding provider-selection
logic — mark that branch as out of scope), (d) parser + edit/plan/build
pipelines, (e) security boundaries, backup, config loading, error
handling, (f) dependency map — Mermaid graph + adjacency table (both,
always; the Provider Layer appears as a single collapsed external
node), (g) risks: bottlenecks, duplication, debt, coupling,
scalability — "none found in <category> after <scope searched>" is a
valid recorded result.
DoD: all 7 checkpoints ✅ with citations; excluded directory declared.
--- P2 → VERIFIED_BUGS.md (target 150–400 lines)
Verify BUG-01, BUG-03, BUG-04 first (BUG-02 recorded once as EXCLUDED
per 0.8 — no verification), then every other IN-SCOPE claim in
test---results/. Archive claims about the Provider Layer: list under a
single "Out-of-Scope claims (not assessed)" table.
Per issue: ID, classification (Section 3), confidence (C1–C4), exact
files/functions/actual lines, root cause, impact, severity (S1–S4).
DoD: all 3 in-scope BUGs + all in-scope archive claims classified;
every C4 has a spawned TSK reference; zero secrets quoted.
--- P3 → NEW_FINDINGS.md (target 150–400 lines)
Fresh review REUSING P1's reading (do not re-read; deepen only where
suspicion exists). Categories: race conditions & threading (ws_handler
/_recv_worker/chunk_queue), async issues, memory leaks, large-context,
in-app streaming (server→frontend), parser ambiguity & mode handling,
error handling, path traversal & security, prompt injection, file
corruption, performance, dead/duplicate code, circular deps.
(Provider/retry/fallback: category removed — out of scope.)
Each finding: FND-ID, severity, confidence, citation. Overlap rule: if
a new finding extends a legacy BUG, document it inside that BUG's entry
in VERIFIED_BUGS.md and only cross-reference here.
DoD: every category has findings or an explicit "none found" line.
--- P4 → MASTER_ROADMAP.md (target 100–250 lines)
Milestones with: Objective, Priority, Difficulty, Estimated sessions,
Dependencies, Risk, Files/functions affected, Acceptance criteria,
Rollback strategy, Testing requirements, DoD.
M1 RULE (conditional, not predetermined): M1 = all IN-SCOPE issues
classified Confirmed with severity S1, from P2 AND P3 combined,
ordered by risk. If no S1 confirmed: M1 = building the
verification/test infrastructure needed to settle the C4 items.
DoD: every milestone complete; M1 justified from actual P2/P3 output;
no milestone touches out-of-scope code.
--- P5 → IMPLEMENTATION_TASKS.md (target 200–500 lines)
Atomic task = ONE of: (type-CODE ≤ ~200 changed LOC, ≤1 session,
independently testable & revertible) or (type-VERIFY: a bounded
investigation with a written conclusion as its deliverable) or
(type-TEST: creating test assets) or (type-DOCS). LOC limits apply to
type-CODE only. All tasks target in-scope files exclusively.
Per task: TSK-ID, type, title(EN), description(AR), reason, files,
functions, est. LOC (CODE only), complexity, risk, dependencies
(TSK IDs), Fixes: BUG/FND IDs, Validated-by: QA-T IDs, expected output,
acceptance tests, regression notes.
DoD: every Confirmed in-scope issue and every C4 item has ≥1 task;
dependency graph has no cycles; task table copied into PROGRESS.md
(status column lives there only).
--- P6 → QA_MASTER_PLAN.md (target 200–400 lines)
Inherit QA-T01–T04 as HISTORICAL baseline (record their scores; mark
them "re-run after M1" — inheriting data is not trusting conclusions;
any of them whose substance is provider behavior → "retired,
out of scope"). Redesign QA-T03 as QA-T03R. Fully specify QA-T05–QA-T10
per CONTEXT. Add QA-T11+ covering: unit, integration, regression,
stress, concurrency, performance, in-app streaming (server→frontend),
large context, all 4 modes, parser, security & path traversal
(sandboxed/localhost only, resource-capped), backup/recovery,
WebSocket reconnect, context builder, E2E. All tests run with the
out-of-scope boundary STUBBED — zero external AI calls, zero cost.
Per test: QA-T ID, purpose, environment constraints, steps, expected
result, failure indicators, success criteria, priority, Validates:
TSK IDs.
DoD: bidirectional traceability complete (spot-check 5 chains
BUG→TSK→QA-T both directions).
--- P7 → FUTURE_IMPROVEMENTS.md (target 80–200 lines)
Production-grade = includes for each item: benefit, cost/complexity,
and prerequisite. Cover: architecture, performance, security, DX,
observability (logging/metrics/tracing), caching, queueing, plugin
system, testing, docs, scalability. Tag each SHORT / MID / LONG term.
(Provider abstraction improvements: excluded — out of scope.)
--- P8 → RELEASE_READINESS_REPORT.md (target 60–150 lines) + final
PROGRESS.md reconciliation.
SPEC: Go/No-Go verdict driven by gates —
G1 all in-scope S1 Confirmed issues have merged fixes + passing QA-T;
G2 in-scope QA-T01–T04 re-run scores ≥ previous scores; G3 QA-T05–T10
executed with pass (stubbed boundary); G4 zero open C4 on in-scope
S1/S2 paths; G5 security tests (QA-T09 family) pass. Each gate:
status + evidence link. Any failed gate ⇒ No-Go + blocking TSK list.
The verdict covers the CORE SYSTEM only and must state this explicitly.
DoD: verdict stated; PROGRESS.md shows P1–P8 ✅ and stage switched to
EXECUTION with M1 tasks ⬜.
==================================================
SECTION 7 — FINAL NOTES
• Phase→Document mapping above is 1:1 and exhaustive; documents ARE the
phase output, not summaries of it.
• "Comprehensive" is defined by each phase's checkpoints + DoD + target
size — nothing more is owed, nothing less accepted — measured against
the IN-SCOPE surface only (0.8).
• When context given in Section 2 conflicts with code: code wins,
discrepancy logged.
• The Provider Layer is a sealed boundary: never read past a call into
it, never task it, never test it beyond stubbing it.
• Never modify source code in MODE A. Never quote secrets anywhere.
